  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Monsoon moisture drives thunderstorms through Thursday,\n   bringing a localized flash flood threat.\n\n-  Hot, dry, and breezy conditions this weekend will be a concern\n   for critical fire weather conditions.\n\n-  Smoke from the Pacific Northwest wildfires will continue to\n   impact air quality, potentially worsening over the weekend. \n\n\nShowers and thunderstorms are developing this afternoon across\nLemhi county into southwest Montana. These storms will be capable\nof producing moderate to heavy rainfall with slow progression to\nthe northeast. The combination of slow moving and heavy rain could\ncreate localized flash flooding conditions. The areas of greatest\nconcern will be the steep terrain around Salmon Idaho and the\nAnaconda/Butte area. \n\nThe monsoonal moisture shifts to the east on Thursday so the\nthreat of heavy rainfall diminishes, but isolated to scattered\nthunderstorms will still be prevalent across the Northern \nRockies. \n\nA low pressure system is still on track to move into the Pacific\nNorthwest Friday into Saturday. Winds aloft will strengthen \nduring this period causing gusty westerly winds during the \nafternoon hours. On Friday, the strongest winds appear to be \nacross west central into northwest Montana. Then, on Saturday, \nthe focus shifts toward north central Idaho into southwest \nMontana. The combination of gusty winds and low minimum humidity \nreadings will lead to near-critical fire weather conditions. \n\nThe long term models indicate that the Northern Rockies will be\nunder southwesterly flow Sunday through much of next week. This\nflow pattern will keep the region warm with afternoon gusty winds.\nThe guidance is showing a lack of moisture moving into the region\nso the threat for afternoon convection is very low.",
